{
    "title": "Authorizing the General Services Administration To Modernize, Expand, and Continue To Operate and Maintain a Pedestrian and Vehicular International Border Crossing at the Lan Luis I Land Port of Entry",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 16, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-16/pdf/2025-23091.pdf",
    "summary": "The President has issued a permit allowing the General Services Administration to modernize, expand, and continue operating the San Luis I Land Port of Entry on the Arizona\u2013Mexico border. The plan adds new northbound lanes, relocates southbound lanes, and updates facilities for both vehicles and pedestrians.\n\nThe permit lays out strict conditions. Construction and operations must follow all federal, state, and local laws, with regular inspections by government agencies. The agency must reduce environmental impacts, secure all necessary permits in advance, and keep the site in good repair. Work cannot begin until the United States finishes formal coordination with Mexico and receives concurrence from the binational water and boundary commission. Major changes, transfers of control, or ownership require presidential approval, and the President may amend or revoke the permit at any time.\n\nWho stands to gain? Travelers, local residents, and cross-border workers could see shorter wait times and safer, more efficient crossings. Local businesses and the regional economy may benefit from smoother traffic and more reliable trade flows. Border personnel could get better tools and space to do their jobs, which can improve service and security.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Nearby communities may face construction noise, detours, and temporary delays. Even with required safeguards, there could be environmental concerns tied to expanded lanes and heavier traffic. Some residents may worry about increased congestion around the port, while taxpayers may question costs and timelines. Those who oppose expanding border infrastructure may also object to the scope of the project. And because the permit requires multiple approvals and coordination steps, delays are possible, which could create uncertainty for businesses and travelers.\n\nIn short, this permit keeps the crossing open while authorizing a significant upgrade under close oversight. The outcome will depend on how well the project manages construction impacts, meets environmental obligations, and delivers on the promise of a faster, safer, and more reliable port of entry.",
